This works. Note: it's fine if a cell's inputs include other compute cells that are in remaining — they'll be processed first. Input cells never end up in `remaining`, so no problem.

Edge case: the callback should fire only once even if multiple dependencies change — yes, because each cell is recomputed exactly once per propagation.

Also, the "callbacks should not be called if dependencies change but the output value doesn't change" test — handled by the value comparison in `_recompute`.

One small point: in the "removing a callback multiple times" test, remove_callback is called 3 times for one callback — if we use list.remove, the second and third calls would raise ValueError! The test does the following:

